[Music]  the  Francis Li grew up in the city of st.  Louis in a very very large family I  think he had 13 siblings grew up in a  very modest middle-class neighborhood in  the southwest part of the city and  what's known as the 23rd board his  father was a politician is also a  restaurateur so he grew up in politics  in 1995 he ran for president the Board  of Aldermen against I think five other  aldermen and he got more votes and all  the rest of them combined became  president of the board in 2001 he ran  for mayor and beat the incumbent mayor  and the former mayor so we beat two  mayors and again had more votes than  both of them combined under mayor slay  there's been sixteen billion dollars in  economic development and that's big  projects like City Arts River and more  modest economic development projects  like the rebirth of these buildings for  the chest hall of fame and the chess  club he also did a lot for people  education was one of his top priorities  he created a process and opened more  than 20 charter schools to give parents  in the city whose kids are either  trapped in bad schools or who would be  forced to move out of the city to get a  good public education give them a choice  and stay in the city of st. Louis I  think he's been very courageous he's  been quite a leader he's faced some very  difficult challenges over the years he's  had problems with fire departments with  unions and he took them all on he always  emerged victorious the city is better  off and you know I think everybody  admires his leadership his  forthrightness is honesty being mayor  was never about him it wasn't about his  interests or what was important to him  it was what is good for this city so  when Rex and Jeanne talked about chess  as a way to engage our children more to  give them more to do after school to  give them something to do that will  engage their mind to give them something  that they can be proud of if they're not  an athlete they're not a football player  a baseball player that appealed to him  well mayor slay was very quickly  supportive he told me some years ago he  says any chess event you have I will  shell a park which I thought was  remarkable dedication that he was well  in tune with what our purpose was with  our effort getting in front kids and  getting into schools need to first very  supportive with that the idea that part  of our brand was going to be that we  were the chest capital of the United  States and one of the most important  places in the world for the game of  chess something you know that attracts  people who are cerebral people who are  strategic people who are college  educated was something that was very  appealing to him and he got it right  away embraced it and you know gladly did  so the Mara was in the Chess Club here a  year or two back and so let's sit down  and play a game I made a move and he  said I'm not sure what to do so I made a  move for him and then I made another  move for myself and then somebody  standing behind him said move the night  and the mayor said which one's the night  I bust out laughin nearly fell off my  chair mayor slay you not only are the  longest-serving there in our city's  history but you are going to go down in  history as the best mayor or city has  ever had our city has been fortunate  that you have been willing to make the  commitment and the sacrifice to be our  mayor for so long you will be sorely  missed and your legacy will be something  that many generations from now people  will still be talking about so good luck  in the next phase of your life and we're  going to miss you as our mayor mayor  slay I would say thank you very much for  your friendship and your leadership  you've meant a lot to me personally even  a lot for all the things that I am  trying to do you've been a big help in  all those efforts and the city is going  to miss you and I'm going to miss you  and the only thing you have left to do  is to learn how to play chess thank  [Music]

